{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 18, :comments ["<b>YHWH shall reign forever and ever.</b> By way of the plain sense (peshat): it says, just as He showed His kingship now in the splitting of the Sea of Reeds — that He saves those who fear Him and destroys those who rebel against Him — so may it be His will to do so in all generations forever. And in the manner of what is written (Psalms 146:10): ״ימלוך ה׳ לעולם אלהיך ציון לדור ודור הללויה״. And ימלך is written deficient (חסר), to teach you that the Holy One, blessed be He, does not come with harsh demands against the creatures, demanding of them hard labor, but rather gently and with soft words, in the manner of what is written (Micah 6:3): ״עמי מה עשיתי לך ומה הלאתיך ענה בי״." "<b>And by way of</b> the Kabbalah: <b>YHWH shall reign forever and ever.</b> After Israel saw the kingship splitting the sea, and they praised the Name that is unique and His kingship, He concluded the praise with the Name that is unique, just as He began with it: ״אשירה לה״. And Onkelos rendered it in his Targum in the present tense, like the language of the verse (Psalms 10:16): ״ה׳ מלך עולם ועד אבדו גוים מארצו״. And perhaps it was Onkelos's intention to translate thus because Moses and all Israel intended in this verse the kingship, and therefore they sealed the song with it; for at the beginning of the words of the song they opened with the Name that is unique — that is, He said ״אשירה לה״ — and now they wished to seal it with the kingship." "<b>And according to</b> this matter, ימלך is written deficient (חסר), because the King is the root of the kingship, and He at times flows abundance into it and at times withholds it, according to the will. And understand this. Or it is further possible that Onkelos's intention in this is that all times — past and future — are in the present before HaShem, may He be exalted, for He preceded the existence of time, and time does not pass beyond Him; therefore he translated the verse in the present tense." "<b>And you should know</b> that the verses of this song are 18 verses. And it is possible to say that they correspond to the 18 vertebrae of the spine, which are destined to be renewed in the revival of the dead and to say song; and this is what the rabbis, of blessed memory, expounded: ״אז ישיר משה ובני ישראל״ — it does not say שר but ישיר; from here is a hint to the revival of the dead from the Torah. And so you will find in the song of the future, which is what Isaiah prophesied (Isaiah 26:1): ״ביום ההוא יושר השיר הזה בארץ יהודה״, until ״יחיו מתיך״ — 18 verses. And just as He mentioned here in this song ״עזי וזמרת יה״, so He mentioned in that song (ibid.): ״בטחו בה׳ עדי עד כי ביה ה׳ צור עולמים״, and He added to the name יה the great, complete Name, to allude to the additional apprehension that will exist at that time beyond all other times — for then HaShem will be complete and the throne will be complete."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:59:47.176688897Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}